To terminate a C program, you can use the `exit()` function. This function is defined in the `stdlib.h` header file. It takes an integer argument, which represents the exit status of the program. If you don't provide any argument, it will exit with a status of 0, indicating successful execution.

Here's an example of how you can use the `exit()` function to terminate a C program:

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>

int main() {
    // Your program logic goes here

    // Terminate the program
    exit(0);

    return 0;
}
```

In this example, the `exit(0)` statement is used to terminate the program with a status of 0. You can replace the `0` with any other integer value to indicate a different exit status. For example, if you want to indicate that there was an error, you can use `exit(1)`.

Remember to include the necessary header files (`stdio.h` and `stdlib.h`) to use the `exit()` function. Additionally, make sure to return 0 from the `main()` function to indicate successful execution. If you don't return anything, the program will still terminate, but it will have an exit status of 1, indicating an error. If you want to explicitly return a value other than 0, you can use the `return` statement instead of calling `exit()`. For example, `return 42;` will exit the program with a status of 42.
